WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.960
Now I'm not actually personally a hardcore singularitarian. Which may surprise some of you.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.640
But I've always been very intrigued by the concept.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:13.300
And I took it on as a sort of a challenge to see
if I could write a meaningful fictional story

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.500
that would be set after the Singularity.
And that led to these books:

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.100
starting with the Quantum Thief and it's sequels.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:28.380
As I was writing them I became aware of
this Russian thinker Nikolai Fedorov

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:37.220
who'd anticipated ideas that sounded very much like the modern idea of the Singularity back in the 1890s.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:41.100
In the Quantum Thief books that was a little side note.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.360
But after finishing the books I kind of was intrigued by it.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.460
I started wondering whether there had been times in the past when it felt like the Singularity was coming.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:56.700
and what did it feel like to live in those times?

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.300
And it turned out that this was very much the case.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.480
The result was my recent novel Summerland

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.680
which just came out.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.120
It is a spy story set in an alternate world where

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.400
a very different kind of singularity has taken place.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.080
Scientists have proven that the afterlife is real
and can be contacted by a radio.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.840
It was inspired by real-life Victorian spiritualists;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.240
ideas from four dimensional geometry;
and etheric physics.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.200
And today I want to tell you more
about all those crazy ideas

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.240
and tell you some stories from real history.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:42.460
And maybe we can discuss all together what those stories can teach us about our present-day worries

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.440
and hopes about the Singularity.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:50.460
So to start let's go back in time 180 years

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:56.500
to 1838 and the invention of the electric telegraph.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:04.100
180 years ago Samuel Morse sent
the first telegram in the United States;

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.660
that was across just two miles of telegraph wire.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:12.440
But the telegraph exploded at an exponential rate

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:19.060
and just ten years later there was 6,000 miles
of telegraph cable in the United States.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.680
6,000 miles in just 10 years.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.600
Tom Standage has called the telegraph network,
"The Victorian Internet."

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.440
I think that's a very good parallel.
There was this explosion of growth.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.200
Wild West period of sort of different competing technologies and different companies.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:41.300
And ultimately a consolidation when
the Western Telegraph Union was formed.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.060
It became sort of a Google of its time.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:50.120
And by the year 1900 it controlled
a million miles of telegraph lines.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:56.780
Now very to the Rochester NY headquarters
of Western Union,

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.200
was a small town called Hyattsville.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:06.080
On March 31, 1848 something quite strange
happened there. These two sisters,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.360
Kate and Margaret Fox, started
receiving messages from the afterlife.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:17.100
The messages came as rapping noises that had a code.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.000
So multiple raps for no; one rap for yes...

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.860
So similar to the Morse code in the telegraph.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:29.120
Initially the girls told their family that the messages came from this creature called Mr. Split Foot.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.440
Maybe the devil. But later changed their story and said

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.220
it was the spirit of a peddler who had
been murdered in their home.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:43.940
It happened that the Fox family was friends
with an influential, radical Quaker family

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:51.580
that lived in Rochester. And they got very excited about the possibility of directly communicating with the dead.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:55.680
And they formed what became
the core of the Spiritualist movement.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.620
And suddenly there was this explosion of mediums.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.460
The Fox sisters got hundreds of imitators. 
They themselves became very famous.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:08.160
Horace Greeley the newspaper man and politician
took them under his wing.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.340
The timing was right for something like this.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:16.480
The US was going through what is now known as the Second Great Awakening.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.680
That was kind of a Protestant religious revival that emphasized a personal relationship with God.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.260
It came with a fascination with
the supernatural and romanticism.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:33.100
So people got very excited when suddenly there was what you could describe as an open source religion.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.700
That promised direct lines of communication
with the dead.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:43.040
Because the early spiritualists were associated with groups like Quakers and Universalists,

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:50.120
they were also very much into social reforms, abolition of slavery, women's rights, and so on.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:57.940
Mediums were mostly women, so that gave women a new kind of voice that helped drive some of their issues.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:02.700
But when I looked into this, what surprised me was

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:09.820
how closely the language used to describe mediums was associated with telegraphy.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:15.920
Here's a period drawing that describes a spiritual telegraph crossing the Atlantic.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:22.140
Some mediums started using
telegraph transmission hardware.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:28.000
First they used existing telegraph hardware
to channel spirits.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.920
And then they started building
their own hardware for contacting spirits.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:39.100
Here's an example of an alphabet wheel for communicating with the dead.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.960
But it's actually modeled after a contemporary design,

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.300
one of the early alphabet wheels that made it easier
for a telegraph operator to read incoming messages.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.960
So there's this interesting feedback loop between
the hardware that the mediums used

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.240
and the hardware that telegraph operators used.

